{"id":"59028cfbad9a26b32d9c227310f8d3972aa6b45f51fa95b37f07d49f956f73cb","lamport":1,"author":"scual.project@gmail.com","created_at":"2026-11-08T20:21:42.908007937Z","format_version":1,"kind":"set-field","payload":{"fields":{"description":"## Parent\n\nSub-issue of 215ee3.\n\n## What to build\n\n`ti reindex` is always safe to run: by default it performs the additive Reindex over every remote ticket id, adding what the index lacks and never rewriting what it has. `ti reindex --reset` keeps today's full rebuild from the remote refs, but first serializes the local store fully and compares it to the remote tracking tree; if they differ it refuses with the number of unsynced changes and the advice to run `ti sync` first or pass `--force`. `--reset --force` proceeds.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] CLI: `ti reindex` on a clone with unsynced local edits and a stranded ticket adds the ticket and keeps the edits, exit 0\n- [ ] CLI: `ti reindex --reset` with unsynced edits exits non-zero, stderr names the count and the two remedies, store untouched\n- [ ] CLI: `ti reindex --reset` with a clean store succeeds as before\n- [ ] CLI: `ti reindex --reset --force` with unsynced edits rebuilds from remote\n- [ ] `--json` output for reindex unchanged in shape apart from any additive field"}}}